course: dictionary
lesson: 7206
title: Who is a pharmacist?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pharmacist is a trained person who prepares and sells medicines. They also explain how to take each one safely. You can find a pharmacist at a pharmacy, which is called a chemist's in the UK, Ireland and Australia, and a drugstore in the US.
explain: «Pharmacists ask about past reactions to medicines so they never hand over one that has made you ill before.» A reaction is how your body responds to something. The pharmacist asks first, so you are kept safe.
explain: «Some medicines are not licensed (approved) for younger children, so pharmacists check the age on the packet.» The word check is the clue: a pharmacist looks carefully before giving you medicine. When you are ill, ask your pharmacist or doctor, and never take medicine just because a friend says it works.
fact: A pharmacist is a trained person who prepares and sells medicines.
fact: A pharmacist explains how to take medicine safely, including how much to take and how often.
fact: A pharmacy, a chemist's and a drugstore are all places where medicines are sold.
fact: A prescription is the written permission from a doctor for a medicine you cannot just buy, and the pharmacist hands it over at the chemist.
fact: «Ignacy Łukasiewicz, a Polish pharmacist,» helped make a kerosene lamp in 1853, which lit a hospital operation.
exercise: «The pharmacist checked the age on the packet.» What does a pharmacist do? | Grows vegetables on a farm | Drives an ambulance to the hospital | Teaches maths in a school | *Prepares and sells medicines and explains how to take them safely
exercise: In the UK, Ireland and Australia, what is another name for a pharmacy? | A post office | *A chemist's | A bakery | A library
exercise: «The doctor may write a prescription.» What is a prescription? | A type of cough sweet | *A written permission for a medicine you cannot just buy | A shop that sells bread | A pill box with the days of the week
test: Why should you ask your pharmacist before taking a new medicine? | Because pharmacists are always too busy to help | So you can skip the instructions | *To find out how to take it safely | To get a free holiday
test: «Pharmacists ask about past reactions to medicines.» Why do they ask? | *So they never hand over one that has made you ill before | To make you laugh | To sell you more medicine | To check your shoe size
exam: In the sentence «the pharmacist at the chemist hands it over», what does pharmacist mean? | A nurse who works only at night | A person who sells paint | *A trained person who prepares and sells medicines | A doctor who performs operations
